This version sorts by the start value, then handles an overlap by keeping the interval with the smaller end. That smaller end is safer because it can fit more later intervals.

When two intervals only touch, such as `[1,2]` and `[2,4]`, they are accepted because the start is equal to the previous end.

```java
class Solution {
    public int MaximumNonOverlappingIntervals(int[][] intervals) {
        if (intervals == null || intervals.length <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        java.util.Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] != b[0]) {
                return Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]);
            }
            return Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]);
        });

        int removed = 0;
        int currentEnd = intervals[0][1];

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
            int start = intervals[i][0];
            int end = intervals[i][1];

            if (start < currentEnd) {
                removed++;
                currentEnd = Math.min(currentEnd, end);
            } else {
                currentEnd = end;
            }
        }

        return removed;
    }
}
```

The array is changed by the in-place sort. An empty array or an array with one interval needs no removal.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, apart from sorting storage.

Instead of counting removals while scanning, this code finds the maximum number of intervals that can stay. Intervals are sorted by end time. For each interval, binary search finds the last interval ending no later than its start.

The DP choice is simple: skip the current interval, or keep it and add one to the best compatible result.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int MaximumNonOverlappingIntervals(vector<vector<int>>& Intervals) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(Intervals.size());
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        sort(Intervals.begin(), Intervals.end(),
             [](const vector<int>& first, const vector<int>& second) {
                 return first[1] < second[1];
             });

        vector<int> ends(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            ends[i] = Intervals[i][1];
        }

        vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            int start = Intervals[i - 1][0];
            int compatible = upper_bound(ends.begin(), ends.begin() + i - 1,
                                         start) - ends.begin();

            int take = 1 + dp[compatible];
            int skip = dp[i - 1];
            dp[i] = max(take, skip);
        }

        return n - dp[n];
    }
};
```

The intervals are sorted in place. If there are no intervals, the early return avoids accessing an empty vector.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the end values and DP table.

Keeping the interval that ends first leaves the most room for the intervals after it. After sorting by end time, an interval that starts before the current end must be removed. Otherwise, it becomes the next interval we keep.

The input vector is sorted in place.

